# 0002. A real-time simulation driven by two background threads

- Status: Accepted
- Date: 2026-11-19

The game runs in real time: one thread brings clients, another closes each month, while the main
thread reads the player's commands.

## Technical details
- **State** (`Model`): budget 500,000, free and rented meters, staff, rent price 700.
- **Clients thread** (`generateClient`): every `RENT_TICK = 20` s, a random number (0 to the
  free meters) rents meters.
- **Month thread** (`environment`): every `MONTH = 60` s, `calculateProfit()` adds income
  (rented × price) minus expenses (0.5 per free meter, 0.8 per rented, `SALARY = 50` per
  employee); the game ends once the budget is at or below `MAX_DEBT = -90000`.
- **Commands**: `1` buy meters (`PARKOMAT_PRICE = 50000` each; needs one employee per 10
  meters), `2` hire staff, `q` quit.
- **Game over**: when the budget drops to `MAX_DEBT`, `environment()` sets the global
  `gameStatus = False` and asks for Enter; the input loop then ends. (Until 2026-11-19 the
  `global` was missing, so the debt limit never ended the game.)

## Technologies and alternatives
- Involved: Python 3 standard library (`threading`, `random`, `time`).
- Alternatives: turn-based steps (simpler and testable, but the lab asked for a live game).
